  <HOMEWORK title="PLL Bring-Up 5: A margin stress test - how noisy a reference can this tolerate?"
            prompt="The loop is fully tuned to its normal values and locks cleanly. Before sign-off, stress it the way a real validation lab would: feed it a deliberately noisy reference and see what actually degrades. A technician watching the lock indicator during this test reports it flickering on and off and calls it a loop stability failure - is that the right read?"
            hint="Don't trust the lock indicator alone under a noisy reference. Average the VCO output frequency over a few seconds instead - is the average still close to reference x N, even while the indicator flickers?"
            explanation="A large amount of timing jitter is injected onto the reference - enough that individual reference edges arrive measurably early or late, which alone is enough to trip a simple instantaneous-phase-error lock detector, even though the loop filter is correctly averaging the jitter out and the VCO's output frequency, averaged over a few seconds, still lands close to the intended reference x N (HOMEWORK_29 demonstrates the same effect in isolation). This is a genuine measurement-methodology lesson for a stress test, not a loop-stability failure: a lock detector built on an instantaneous threshold can read as unlocked even while the loop is doing its job correctly, if the thing it's watching is itself noisy for a reason that has nothing to do with loop stability. The real action item from a result like this is upstream - tightening whatever is injecting that much jitter onto the reference - not retuning the loop filter."/>
</LABCHAIN>
